BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:2026-11-20-mutlu@cs-futures-ai.wisc.edu
DTSTAMP:20261113T222401Z
DTSTART:20261120T210000Z
DTEND:20261120T230000Z
SUMMARY:Bilge Mutlu: What Should I Still Do Myself? Judgment\, labor\, and 
 AI in research
DESCRIPTION:CS Futures in the Age of AI seminar series\, hosted by the CS A
 I Council.\nSpeaker: Bilge Mutlu\n\nAbstract: AI can now write code\, surv
 ey a literature\, run an analysis\, and draft a paper. That raises a quest
 ion for everyone who does research\, at every stage: what should I still d
 o myself? For students\, the labor AI now does is how researchers used to 
 build judgment\, the ability to tell whether a result is right\, whether i
 t is interesting\, and what to do next. For faculty and staff\, the questi
 on is how to supervise work we never did\, what to ask of the people we me
 ntor\, and where our own lines are. This talk grew out of questions CS stu
 dents raised after an earlier version. Where does judgment stop and implem
 entation start? When is the work still yours\, and what should you disclos
 e? How do you build judgment before you have it? Drawing on how other fiel
 ds train judgment\, and on examples from student work and my own practice\
 , I'll offer a working rule for what to keep for yourself\, a way to pract
 ice alongside AI without losing the skill\, and the norms we might set tog
 ether as a department. The first hour is the talk\; the second hour is ope
 n discussion.\n\nhttps://wrightstephen.github.io/cs-futures-ai/#2026-11-20
 -mutlu
LOCATION:WARF Seminar Room\, 7th Floor\, Morgridge Hall\, UW–Madison
URL:https://wrightstephen.github.io/cs-futures-ai/
END:VEVENT
